Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=82.5^\circ$. Dividing by secant multiplies by cosine because $1/\sec x=\cos x$. The resulting pair at $x=165^\circ$ permits a second application.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{\sin\left(165^\circ\right)}{\sec\left(165^\circ\right)}\\&=\sin\left(165^\circ\right)\cos\left(165^\circ\right)\\&=\frac{1}{2}\,\sin\left(330^\circ\right)
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(330^\circ\right)=- \frac{1}{2}$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{1}{2}\,\left(- \frac{1}{2}\right)\\&=- \frac{1}{4}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=11.25^\circ$ in the denominator. Use $\sec x=1/\cos x$, then apply the double-angle identity again at $x=22.5^\circ$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{2\,\sec\left(22.5^\circ\right)}{\sin\left(22.5^\circ\right)}\\&=\frac{2}{\sin\left(22.5^\circ\right)\cos\left(22.5^\circ\right)}\\&=\frac{4}{\sin\left(45^\circ\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(45^\circ\right)=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$.
Rationalize the denominator by multiplying numerator and denominator by $\sqrt{2}$:
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{4}{\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)}\\&=\frac{8}{\sqrt{2}}\\&=4 \sqrt{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

